# Splitting conductive water: two gases, in a two-to-one ratio
#
# Pure water conducts too poorly for this apparatus and is still refused. A
# little sodium sulfate carries current without being preferentially discharged:
# water becomes hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode.
register lv2
add v1 water 200mL
add v1 Na2SO4 1g
# Keep the cell open: collecting hydrogen and oxygen together would make an
# ignitable mixture, and gas collection is not needed to compare their amounts.
electrolyse v1 0.5A 30min
look v1
inspect v1

# Faraday's law owns the amounts: two electrons per H2 and four per O2, hence
# twice as much hydrogen. This is an undivided teaching cell with inert
# electrodes. It does not model electrode overpotential, gas crossover, a
# membrane, ignition, efficiency or the voltage needed to drive the current.
